I have been thinking about golems.  Wizards tend to use them as
servants and guards, they don't eat nor drink, and won't complain about
their existence.  So, what if players could create golems, provided
they had the materials and resources needed.  Here is some "recipes"
that occurred to me.  Also, the "Emet"(truth) & "Meit"(death)
engravings could lead to more interesting gameplay, in the potential
sense.  For example, trying to destroy enemy golems by trying to make
it die.

STONE GOLEM
Find a boulder, and engrave into it "Emet".  Use a wand of undead
turning.  It is complete, and the golem is yours to command.  However,
burned engraving is preferred, because otherwise the golem would
deactivate with time or an enemy could smudge out "Emet" and replace it
with "Meit", which deactivates the golem.  This applies to all created
golems.  When the golem is activated, it would be neutral.  You would
have to use some means of assuming control over the golem.

FLESH GOLEM
Find a dead body and turn to stone.  Engrave into it "Emet".  Return to
flesh, and use wand of undead turning, which infuses into the body
life.  It is now animated, but not under your command.  Alternatively,
a boulder may be first engraved and then  turned into flesh, providing
the flesh required for the golem.

I think this sort of concept could be expanded upon and improved,
though balancing the golem creation process would be a crucial part of
this.  Some immediate ideas that come to mind for making the process
more complicated is to add in a requirement for a wand of polymorph.
In any case, please discuss this idea if it interests you.

